WHAT CHANGED AND WHY, 2026-08-12. Until this revision the file was headed
`brotli — provenance note`, i.e. a record carrying no condition, and it said the
google/brotli C library "was used only as an oracle to generate byte-exact test
vectors". That sentence was false about the files actually committed. The 34
files under `src/testdata/` were not generated by exercising an oracle; they are
byte-for-byte copies of `tests/testdata/*` from github.com/google/brotli,
verified by downloading upstream and running `cmp` (`quickfox`,
`quickfox.compressed` and the rest of the corpus). `alice29.txt` gives the point
away on its own: it is the Canterbury Corpus text google/brotli vendors, and no
oracle "generates" it. Reproduced third-party test data owes attribution exactly
as ported source does — the same shape as `bech32`, `csvstream`, `decimal`,
`json5` and `xml`, and the same shape as the `bitcoinscript` defect the
2026-07-31 sweep found.

